[[George Michael]]'s inspiration for the song was a scribbled note that his bandmate [[Andrew Ridgeley]] had left for his parents, intended to read "wake me up before you go" but with "up" accidentally written twice, so Ridgeley wrote "go" twice on purpose. In 1984, Michael had this to say on the development of the song:

{{blockquote|I just wanted to make a really energetic pop record that had all the best elements of Fifties and Sixties records, combined with our attitude and our approach, which is obviously more uptempo and a lot younger than some of those records. It's one of those tracks that gets rid of a lot of your own personal influences; it reminds me of so many different records that I couldn't actually nail them down. I'd done a demo at home that just had a bass line and a vocal on it. Usually, I write the record in my head; I know what all the parts are going to be and I sing them to all our musicians. And it was great. ... We actually did it as a rehearsal. We used a [[LinnDrum]] because the drummer was late, and it was such a good track that we kept it.<ref>{{cite magazine|first= Christopher |last= Connelly |author-link= Chris Connelly (journalist) |title= Wham! A wake-up call to the U.S. |magazine= [[Rolling Stone]] |issue= 433 |date= 25 October 1984 |page= 48}}</ref>}}

It was recorded within two days at [[Sarm West|Sarm West Studio{{nbs}}2]] in London, with a live rhythm section.<ref name=FaithSOS/> The song, according to Michael, had been done in one take without any drop-ins (overdubs) at {{nowrap|all{{px2}}{{mdash}}{{px2}}}}‌production-wise, he noted that the two options to him were "either to be like [[Trevor Horn]] and go for stunning sounds on your own, or just get a great sound on each instrument and go for a live take".<ref>{{cite magazine|url=https://worldradiohistory.com/Archive-All-Audio/Archive-Modern-Recording/80s/Modern-Recording-1985-06.pdf|date=June 1985|access-date=May 1, 2021|via=worldradiohistory.com|magazine=Modern Recording & Music|title=Making it big as they go-go!|author= Thomas, David|pages=32–36}}</ref> The UK sleeve was designed by [[Peter Saville (graphic designer)|Peter Saville]], with photography by [[Trevor Key]].<ref>{{cite web |url= http://www.petersaville.info/sleeves/1982-1984.html |publisher= Petersaville.info |access-date= 29 March 2014 |title= Sleeve designed by Peter Saville: Sleeves 1982-1984 |archive-url= https://web.archive.org/web/20130521120043/http://www.petersaville.info/sleeves/1982-1984.html |archive-date= 21 May 2013 |url-status= dead }}</ref>

